{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And he shall slaughter, and the priests shall offer</b> — from reception onward it is a priestly service; this teaches that slaughter is valid by a non-priest. <b>And they shall offer</b> — this is reception, and its meaning conveys carrying; we have learned that both of them are performed by the sons of Aaron — so Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 1:5). But this is not correct. Rather, the midrash of our Sages (Torat Kohanim, parashah 4:4): \"And they shall offer\" — this is the reception of the blood, for the expression \"and they shall offer\" does not refer to the bringing of the blood to the altar, which is the carrying, but rather it is an expression of קָרְבָּן, that they should make of the blood a קָרְבָּן, namely to receive it and to dash it upon the altar. For Scripture mentioned with regard to the owner of the offering the bringing and the leaning and the slaughter, and after the slaughter it immediately mentioned \"the sons of Aaron\" — if so, then even the reception itself is a priestly service and may be performed only by a fit priest and with a service vessel, and all the more so the carrying and the dashing. Furthermore, the carrying of the limbs to the ramp is disqualified by a non-priest, for thus they derived (Zevaḥim 4a): \"And the priest shall offer the whole and burn it upon the altar\" (Leviticus 1:13) — this refers to the carrying of the limbs to the ramp. If so, then even the carrying of the blood requires a priest in his priestly capacity."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The citation זבחים ד refers to Zevaḥim 4a; Ramban cites Talmud pages by numeral without the amud letter."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:49:26.387617969Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}